  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Prakash Nadesan vs State Of Kerala on 1 April, 2012 Author: A.V.Ramakrishna Pillai Bench: A.V.Ramakrishna Pillai In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice A.V.Ramakrishna Pillai Friday, The 12Th Day Of June 2015/22Nd Jyaishta, 1937 WP(C).No. 17691 of 2015 (J)",
    "facts": "Aggrieved by the inaction on the part of the respondent panchayath in taking a decision on the application submitted by the petitioner, the petitioner has come up before this Court. 2. The petitioner is the working partner of Spice 7 Restaurant within the local limits of the 2nd respondent panchayath. The petitioner's firm has been conducting the said restaurant with all the required licence since 2011 onwards and Ext.P4 trade licence was issued by the respondent panchayath for 2014-15. The petitioner applied for trade licence for the year, 2015-16, as evidenced by Ext.P5 receipt; and the respondent panchayath issued Ext.P7 notice intimating that a complaint is received from the the local people alleging that the waste water from the petitioner's restaurant is ..2.. contaminating the water of their well. According to the petitioner, he has erected waste water treatment plant in the premises, which conforms to the standard prescribed by the Pollution Control Board. According to him, no waste water is discharged from the restaurant to the adjacent lands and the treated waste water is used for the purpose of watering plants in the petitioner's compound and there is no question of contamination of any drinking water in the well arises.",
    "arguments": "The petitioner's grievance is that so far, no action whatsoever is taken on the trade licence application of the petitioner. He further alleges that since the restaurant is functioning without trade licence, certain local people, having vested interest, are attempting to close down the restaurant. According to the petitioner, the non consideration of trade licence application is dereliction of statutory duty caste on the respondent as per the Kerala Panchayath Raj Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "The petitioner's grievance is that so far, no action whatsoever is taken on the trade licence application of the petitioner. He further alleges that since the restaurant is functioning without trade licence, certain local people, having vested interest, are attempting to close down the restaurant. According to the petitioner, the non consideration of trade licence application is dereliction of statutory duty caste on the respondent as per the Kerala Panchayath Raj Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
